        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "779ac190-7741-5f51-a3cf-9fd74f4cdee8",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Max Ehrmann, Les Crane, Joe Pyne, Phil Donahue, Oprah Winfrey\n- Works: Desiderata, The Oprah Winfrey Show\n- Topics: tabloid talk shows, spoken-word recordings\n\nFacts:\n- Max Ehrmann was an American writer, poet, and attorney from Terre Haute, Indiana, who was born on September 26, 1872, and died on September 9, 1945.\n- Max Ehrmann wrote the 1927 prose poem \"Desiderata,\" whose Latin title means \"things desired\" or \"desired things.\"\n- Tabloid talk shows originated in the 1960s and early 1970s with series hosted by Joe Pyne, Les Crane, and Phil Donahue.\n- Les Crane was a radio announcer and television talk show host who was born on December 3, 1933, and died on July 13, 2008.\n- Les Crane was a pioneer in interactive broadcasting.\n- \"Desiderata\" was largely unknown during Max Ehrmann's lifetime.\n- Les Crane's 1971 spoken-word recording of \"Desiderata\" became a hit and won a Grammy for Best Spoken Word.\n- \"Desiderata\" became widely known after its use in devotional and spoken-word recordings in 1971 and 1972.\n- \"The Oprah Winfrey Show\" debuted nationally in 1986 and popularized the tabloid talk show format with personal confession-filled content.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "9191d47a-0fe8-5a59-8769-7626d7ab920f",
        "text": "Page: Max Ehrmann. Sentence: Max Ehrmann (September 26, 1872 – September 9, 1945) was an American writer, poet, and attorney from Terre Haute, Indiana, widely known for his 1927 prose poem \"Desiderata\" (Latin: \"things desired\").\nPage: Desiderata. Sentence: \"Desiderata\" (Latin: \"desired things\") is a 1927 prose poem by American writer Max Ehrmann.\nPage: Desiderata. Sentence: Largely unknown in the author's lifetime, the text became widely known after its use in devotional and spoken-word recordings in 1971 and 1972.\nPage: Les Crane. Sentence: Les Crane (born Lesley Stein; December 3, 1933 – July 13, 2008) was a radio announcer and television talk show host, a pioneer in interactive broadcasting who also scored a spoken word hit with his 1971 recording of the poem \"Desiderata\", winning a \"Best Spoken Word\" Grammy.\nPage: Tabloid talk show. Sentence: Airing mostly during the day and distributed mostly through television syndication, tabloid talk shows originated in the 1960s and early 1970s with series hosted by Joe Pyne, Les Crane and Phil Donahue; the format was popularized by personal confession-filled \"The Oprah Winfrey Show\", which debuted nationally in 1986.",
